Here is a brief breakdown of each file included in the Smart Inventory system:

1. Backend Code
main.py (Flask App): The core backend file built with Python and Flask. It initializes the SQLite3 database (inventory.db), defines database tables (products, history, and notifications), handles routes for the dashboard, product management, AI forecasting, reports, CSV exports, and runs the application.  
PY
+ 1

2. Frontend HTML Templates
add_product.html: The web form page that allows users to add a new item to the inventory by entering its name, category, quantity, and price.  
HTML

dashboard.html: The main control center displaying high-level statistics, low stock alert banners, a notification dropdown menu, search tools, and a full product inventory table with quick stock adjusters.  
HTML

edit_product.html: The form page used to update existing product attributes such as name, category, quantity, and price.  
HTML

forecast.html: The AI demand forecasting page that features visual charts for stock depletion projections and calculates daily burn rates, stockout runtimes, and recommended Economic Order Quantities (EOQ).  
HTML

products.html: A dedicated page for viewing, searching, and managing all items currently in stock, complete with direct links to edit or delete products.  
HTML

reports.html: An analytics page summarizing inventory metrics, total stock valuation, category breakdowns, a CSV export option, and a real-time system activity log.  
HTML

settings.html: A configuration page displaying system metadata, inventory rules, environment stacks, and a theme toggle for switching between dark and light modes.  
HTML

3. Frontend Scripts & Styles
main.js: A client-side JavaScript file that controls interactive elements like animated stat counters, live search filters, table sorting, theme persistence, and asynchronous notification fetching.  
JS

style.css: The master stylesheet providing a 3D glassmorphic dark theme, responsive layouts for mobile and desktop devices, status badges, and styled form components.  
CSS

explain app

The Smart Inventory application is a full-stack, web-based inventory management system. It is designed to help small-to-medium businesses track stock levels, manage product details, monitor low-stock thresholds, and analyze overall inventory performance.

Core Features & Architecture
Backend Flask Server (app.py): Powered by Python and Flask, it handles server routing, database queries, and CSV data exports.  
PY

SQLite Database (inventory.db): Manages structured tables for storing products, audit logs (history), and system notifications.  
PY

Interactive Dashboard & Views: Built with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript using a modern 3D glassmorphic dark interface (with a light-mode toggle). It includes real-time search filtering, dynamic stat counters, and mobile-responsive navigation.  
HTML
+ 4

Advanced Operations:

Low Stock Management: Automatically flags items running low (quantity ≤5) and features a drop-down notification center.  
HTML
+ 1

Quick Stock Adjusters: Allows users to increase or decrease quantities instantly right from the main tables.  
HTML

AI Demand Forecasting: Calculates inventory depletion projections, daily burn rates, and recommended Economic Order Quantities (EOQ).  
HTML

Reports & Logging: Tracks cumulative inventory dollar values, product categories, and a real-time system activity log.  
HTML

explain each code in app.py

Here is a breakdown of the key functions and blocks of code inside app.py:

Imports & Configuration:

Imports necessary Flask components (Flask, render_template, request, redirect, url_for, Response), SQLite3, and CSV modules.  
PY

Instantiates the Flask app and defines the database filename (inventory.db).  
PY

Database Connection & Initialization (get_db & init_db):

get_db(): Connects to the SQLite database and configures rows to be accessed like dictionaries (sqlite3.Row).  
PY

init_db(): Creates the core tables if they do not already exist: products (storing item details), history (for audit logging), and notifications (for low-stock tracking).  
PY

Main Dashboard Route (/):

Handles product searches by name or category, calculates overall metrics (total products, total stock, low stock count, and total inventory value), and renders the dashboard.html template.  
PY

Notification API Endpoints (/api/notifications/...):

/api/notifications/fetch: Checks items running low (≤5 units), generates unread alerts if missing, and returns current notification counts.  
PY

/api/notifications/read/<int:id>: Marks a specific notification alert as read in the database.  
PY

Product Management Routes (/products, /add-product, /edit-product, /delete-product):

/products: Lists and filters all items across the inventory.  
PY

/add-product: Handles GET requests to load the add form and POST requests to insert new products while recording an audit log entry in the history table.  
PY

/edit-product/<int:id>: Retrieves a product by ID and updates its properties on form submission.  
PY

/delete-product/<int:id>: Removes a product from the database and logs the deletion action.  
PY

Analytics, Forecast, & Settings Routes:

/reports: Gathers aggregated category stats, inventory value summaries, and recent activity logs for display.  
PY

/forecast: Simulates AI burn rates, stockout runways, and Economic Order Quantities (EOQ) for predictive inventory planning.  
PY

/settings: Renders the configuration page.  
PY

Stock Adjustment & CSV Export:

/adjust-stock/<int:id>/<action>: Incrementally increases or decreases a product's quantity directly via quick action buttons.  
PY

/export-csv: Queries all products and dynamically streams them as a downloadable CSV report file (inventory_report.csv).  
PY

Data Seeding & Server Startup:

seed_realistic_data(): Inserts sample starter items (e.g., laptops, chairs) if the inventory table is completely empty.  
PY

if __name__ == "__main__":: Initializes the database, seeds sample data, and starts the Flask development server.  
PY

